const body = `<h1>9.4 — Your Scene Is Data: Metadata &amp; Files</h1>

<p class="lesson-lead">
    Every scene you have saved is a text file. So is every prefab, every
    animator, and every tile. So is the small file sitting beside each sprite
    that you have never once opened. Today you read those files instead of
    trusting the Inspector — and you find out how a scene that never writes the
    word "Coin" still knows exactly which coin it means.
</p>

${renderAskBanner('how do I find out what is inside a file?', 3, 'concept')}

${renderDecompose('9.4', DECOMPOSE, 3)}

<!-- ============================================================ -->
<div class="activity expand-app">
    <div class="activity-header">
        <span class="activity-label">Concept Lab</span>
        <span class="activity-time">~15 min</span>
    </div>

    <p><strong>Step 1 — look in the folder, not in Unity.</strong> Open
    <code>Assets/_Scenes/</code> in Finder or Explorer. There are exactly three
    files: <code>Start.unity</code>, <code>Overworld.unity</code> and
    <code>Platformer.unity</code>. That is the whole game. Those three are also
    the three entries in <code>ProjectSettings/EditorBuildSettings.asset</code>,
    in that order — indices 0, 1 and 2 — which is what
    <code>SceneManager.LoadScene(scene)</code> in <code>SceneSwitch.cs</code>
    is indexing into. Nobody ever types the word "Overworld" into that code, and
    it still loads the right scene.</p>

    <p><strong>Step 2 — open one in a text editor.</strong> Not Unity. Right-click
    <code>Overworld.unity</code> and open it with TextEdit, Notepad or VS Code.
    It is YAML: human-readable <code>key: value</code> text. Every object in the
    scene is one block, and every block starts the same way — a Unity class id,
    an <code>&amp;</code>, and a file id that is unique inside that one file:</p>
    <pre><code>--- !u!1 &amp;6040774585044851471
GameObject:
  m_Name: Turret
  m_TagString: Enemy
  m_Component:
  - component: {fileID: 4844124505973536318}
--- !u!212 &amp;-8321157105715108983
SpriteRenderer:
  m_Sprite: {fileID: 1391597418, guid: 465f5cb57952b46b387408a348ba8589, type: 3}
  m_SortingLayer: 4</code></pre>

    <p>The number after <code>!u!</code> is the KIND of thing, and the project
    uses only a handful of kinds, all of them readable in the file itself:</p>
    <table>
        <tr><th>Anchor</th><th>What that block is</th></tr>
        <tr><td><code>!u!1</code></td><td>A GameObject — the thing with a name and a tag.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>!u!4</code> / <code>!u!224</code></td><td>A Transform, or a RectTransform when the object is on a Canvas.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>!u!212</code></td><td>A SpriteRenderer — the component that points at your artwork.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>!u!114</code></td><td>A MonoBehaviour — one of YOUR scripts, named by a guid rather than by a filename.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>!u!95</code></td><td>An Animator. <code>!u!50</code> is a Rigidbody2D and <code>!u!61</code> a BoxCollider2D.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>!u!1001</code></td><td>A prefab instance — one of the objects you dragged in from the Project window. This is the one that matters most today.</td></tr>
    </table>

    <p><strong>Step 3 — find your own turret.</strong> There is no turret in
    <code>Platformer.unity</code> and none in <code>Start.unity</code>: search
    either one for the word "Turret" and you get nothing. The turrets are in the
    Overworld, under an object named <code>Turrets</code>, and each one is a
    <code>!u!1001</code> block. That block does not contain a turret. It contains
    a guid, a short list of changes, and nothing else — because the turret itself
    lives in <code>Assets/Prefabs/Turret.prefab</code>, and the scene is only
    keeping a note about it.</p>

    <p><strong>Step 4 — the file beside the file.</strong> Every asset in the
    project has a second file next to it with <code>.meta</code> on the end —
    every sprite, every script, every prefab, and every folder including
    <code>_Scenes</code>. Open <code>Assets/Prefabs/Turret.prefab.meta</code>.
    It is seven lines:</p>
    <pre><code>fileFormatVersion: 2
guid: 54f2fe71921990e48b811a0b3b35835f
PrefabImporter:
  externalObjects: {}
  userData:
  assetBundleName:
  assetBundleVariant:</code></pre>

    <p>That is the entire file. The asset next to it holds the turret; the
    <code>.meta</code> holds the turret's <em>name</em> as far as the rest of the
    project is concerned — the guid, a 32-character number that is unique across
    every project on earth. The <code>.png</code> sidecar is the same three
    lines followed by two hundred and seventy-one more: texture type, pixels per
    unit, pivot, filter mode, compression, and the rectangle of every sprite
    sliced out of the image. All of that is <strong>metadata</strong> —
    description, not content. The picture does not know any of it.</p>

    <div class="callout info">
        <strong>Data and metadata are two different files here — and you can
        prove it.</strong> Rename <code>Shovel.png</code> to
        <code>ShovelOld.png</code>, open Unity, and look at the player. Nothing
        moved, nothing went pink. The <code>.meta</code> kept its guid, so every
        reference still resolves; the filename was only ever a label on the
        folder listing. Now delete the <code>.meta</code> instead, and every
        scene that pointed at that sprite loses its connection, because the guid
        those references were written against is gone. The identity of an asset
        is not its name, and it is not its pixels. It is the number in the
        sidecar.
    </div>

    <p><strong>Step 5 — follow one reference all the way.</strong> This is the
    whole lesson in four lines. In <code>Overworld.unity</code>, search for
    <code>stripped</code>. You will find blocks like this one:</p>
    <pre><code>--- !u!4 &amp;861464021 stripped
Transform:
  m_CorrespondingSourceObject: {fileID: 4281311786053241907, guid: 69c7343f050914c7787bbbe42c07b5b8, type: 3}
  m_PrefabInstance: {fileID: 186509170}</code></pre>

    <p>Read it as a sentence: <em>the Transform you are looking for is not
    written here. It lives in the asset whose guid is
    <code>69c7343f050914c7787bbbe42c07b5b8</code>, as the object numbered
    <code>4281311786053241907</code> inside that asset.</em> Now go and check
    it. <code>Assets/Prefabs/Coin.prefab.meta</code> carries that exact guid, and
    <code>Assets/Prefabs/Coin.prefab</code> contains
    <code>--- !u!4 &amp;4281311786053241907</code>. You have just walked from a
    scene, through a sidecar, into a prefab, using nothing but a number. That is
    what <strong>serialization</strong> means in Unit 9: the object in memory
    has been written down as text, and the pointer between two objects has been
    written down as a guid instead of a memory address.</p>

    <div class="socratic">
        <h3>Socratic</h3>
        <p>
            A prefab is a file, and a scene holds instances of it. Explain
            "prefab as data abstraction": what does the prefab FILE hide from
            the scene, and what does its guid GUARANTEE that a file path cannot?
            Test it — rename a sprite and count what breaks, then find a
            reference written by guid and count again.
        </p>
    </div>
</div>

<!-- ============================================================ -->
<div class="activity expand-app">
    <div class="activity-header">
        <span class="activity-label">Build Lab</span>
        <span class="activity-time">~20 min</span>
    </div>

    <p><strong>Step 1 — change a value without opening Unity.</strong> Make a
    copy of <code>Overworld.unity</code> first. Then find the block
    <code>--- !u!1001 &amp;208023028</code> — one of the two turret instances —
    and read its override list. One entry changes
    <code>m_LocalPosition.x</code> to <code>15.13</code>. Now open
    <code>Assets/Prefabs/Turret.prefab</code> and read the turret's own root
    Transform, <code>--- !u!4 &amp;4844124505973536318</code>:
    <code>m_LocalPosition: {x: 0, y: 0, z: 0}</code>. The prefab says the turret
    is at zero; the scene says <em>this</em> turret is at 15.13. That is what an
    override is — a value on the instance, not on the asset.</p>

    <p>Now change <code>15.13</code> to something absurd, close Unity first if
    it is open, reopen the scene, and press Play. The turret has moved. You
    edited the game without opening the Inspector, which is the same power as
    the palette edit from <a href="9.2.html">9.2</a>, one level deeper.</p>

    <p><strong>Step 2 — diff a playtest edit.</strong> Close Unity. Copy
    <code>Overworld.unity</code> again, open Unity, move three objects, save,
    close. Now compare the two files — VS Code's compare, or just search for the
    changed numbers — and notice how small the diff is. Each object you moved is
    a handful of changed <code>m_LocalPosition</code> values inside one
    <code>!u!1001</code> block, one number per axis. Your entire semester of
    level design is that readable.</p>

    <p><strong>Step 3 — find the metadata that is out of date.</strong> Stay in
    the turret instance and read the <em>first</em> entry in the same override
    list:</p>
    <pre><code>- target: {fileID: -28657926046577547, guid: 54f2fe71921990e48b811a0b3b35835f, type: 3}
  propertyPath: m_IsTrigger
  value: 0</code></pre>

    <p>That says: <em>in the asset whose guid is 54f2 — the turret prefab — find
    the object numbered -28657926046577547 and set its <code>m_IsTrigger</code>
    to 0.</em> So go and find it. Search <code>Turret.prefab</code> for
    <code>28657926046577547</code> and you get zero hits. Search the whole
    project and you get one hit: this line, the one looking for it. There is no
    such component. The prefab has six anchors and none of them is that number,
    and the prefab holds no collider at all — so an entry that claims to be
    setting a collider's trigger flag is describing an object that does not
    exist. The Player instance carries the same kind of ghost:
    <code>m_GravityScale</code>, aimed at a number that is an anchor nowhere in
    the project.</p>

    <p>This is not a bug you have to fix, and it is the best thing in the file.
    <strong>Somebody edited these files as text — the prefabs and the scenes —
    and never re-saved them in Unity.</strong> The scene still remembers the
    version of the prefab it was written against, and that version had a
    collider. Unity, when it next opens and saves that scene, will quietly drop
    the dead entry, because Unity reads the prefab to find out what exists. A
    text file cannot. That is the difference between a file that is true and a
    file that is merely old, and it is why the same scene file behaves
    differently after somebody re-saves it.</p>

</div>

<!-- ============================================================ -->

<div class="checklist">
    <h3>Checkpoint — before moving on, you must be able to:</h3>
    <ul>
        <li>Open a scene, prefab or animator as text and find a specific object's data</li>
        <li>Say what a <code>.meta</code> file holds and why the asset's identity is in it</li>
        <li>Follow a guid from a <code>.meta</code> into a scene reference and back</li>
        <li>Explain <code>[SerializeField]</code> as serialization in both directions</li>
        <li>Tell the data apart from the metadata that describes it</li>
        <li>Recognise a stale override and say what made it stale</li>
        <li>Break this topic into parts small enough to look each one up on its own, and say in one line what each part does</li>
        <li>Say which earlier lesson each part came from, and where you would reread it</li>
    </ul>
</div>

